# ─── ANDROID SIGNING MATERIAL — NEVER COMMIT ANY OF THIS ───────────────────────────────────
#
# Added 2026-09-13, at the moment this repository acquired a release key of its own. Until then
# the published APK was signed with the Android DEBUG key (verified on the live v1.1.20 artefact:
# `CN=Android Debug, O=Android, C=US`), which is auto-generated per machine — so there was no
# signing material to protect and no rule to state.
#
# There is now, and the consequence of committing it is absolute rather than embarrassing: an
# Android app can NEVER change its signing certificate. Every handset refuses an update signed by
# a different key. A leaked key cannot be rotated away from — it can only be used by somebody else
# to publish an app the fleet will accept as genuine.
#
# The canonical copy lives in the GitHub Actions secrets (base64) and in whatever the institution
# uses for long-term custody. The working copy below is a convenience for a local release build
# and is not the source of truth.
*.p12
*.jks
*.keystore
fieldrepo-release.*
keystore.properties
KEYSTORE-CUSTODY.md

# Scratch files the CI check steps write into the repository root. They are produced by
# .github/workflows/checks.yml, and running one of those steps by hand — which is the fastest way to
# reproduce a red gate, and how the `bash -e` defects of 2026-09-14 were found — drops them here.
doc-problems.txt
known-doc-problems.txt
docs-check.log
pytest-output.log
actual-failures.txt
expected-failures.txt
